WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.500
How do you design a

2
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.340
semaphore protocol? First separate shared

3
00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.960
data, resource capacity and ordering

4
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.880
dependencies. Those are different responsibilities,

5
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.640
so initializing every semaphore to

6
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.900
one is not a design

7
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.880
method, however tidy the code

8
00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:16.280
may look.

9
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.300
For shared mutable data, identify

10
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.880
the critical section and protect

11
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.400
it with a binary semaphore

12
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.900
initially one. Wait before the

13
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.920
protected update and signal after

14
00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.940
it. Keep the protected region

15
00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:30.360
focused on the data that

16
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.740
must remain consistent.

17
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.240
For a resource pool, initialize

18
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:35.500
a counting semaphore to the

19
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.860
number of available instances. Three

20
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.660
identical printers begin with three

21
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.300
permits, even if only two

22
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.540
jobs currently exist. The initial

23
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.740
count describes capacity, not the

24
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.060
current number of customers.

25
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.160
A job waits for a

26
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.620
printer permit before using a

27
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.000
printer and signals when that

28
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.620
printer is returned. Three successful

29
00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:55.980
waits can allocate the three

30
00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.620
instances; a fourth requester blocks.

31
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:00.880
No single permit should represent

32
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.860
two simultaneous users of one

33
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:03.300
printer.

34
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.380
The shared job list needs

35
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:07.040
its own mutex while entries

36
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.120
are removed or updated. Using

37
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.560
only printers equal to three

38
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.180
would allow three concurrent list

39
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.320
mutations and could race. Using

40
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:15.820
one mutex around the entire

41
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.140
print would protect the list

42
00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.240
but unnecessarily serialize the printer

43
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:19.460
pool.

44
00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.620
For ordering, suppose statement B

45
00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.600
must occur only after statement

46
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.320
A in another process. Initialize

47
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.060
an ordering semaphore to zero,

48
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.420
signal after A, and wait

49
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.080
before B. If the signal

50
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.280
arrives first, the permit remembers

51
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.480
that A has occurred.

52
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.000
That one-way dependency is not

53
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.280
mutual exclusion around an arbitrary

54
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.420
shared object. Its zero initialization

55
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.640
intentionally prevents B from starting

56
00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.940
before A. A mutex normally

57
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.000
starts with one permit so

58
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.480
the first contender can enter

59
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.520
an otherwise free critical section.

60
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.680
Incorrect acquisition order can deadlock:

61
00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.920
P zero takes lock S

62
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.740
and waits for Q, while

63
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.120
P one takes Q and

64
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.840
waits for S. Both waits

65
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.720
are individually legitimate, but the

66
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.860
combined resource dependencies form a

67
00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.320
cycle. Consistent ordering or another

68
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.060
justified protocol is needed.

69
00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.000
Queue discipline matters too. A

70
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.800
priority queue may keep favoring

71
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.380
new high-priority arrivals, while a

72
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.600
last-in-first-out list can bury an

73
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.540
old waiter. Preserving a resource

74
00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.120
count does not by itself

75
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.700
prove bounded waiting for each

76
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:28.120
process.

77
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:29.920
Things to take note of.

78
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.100
State what every semaphore means,

79
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.080
derive its initial value from

80
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.860
that meaning, and pair each

81
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.700
acquisition with the appropriate release.

82
00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.040
Protect the data, count the

83
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.940
instances and enforce the dependency;

84
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.840
do not interchange those jobs

85
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:44.700
without an argument.
